Column H is headed "1st Product wt highest cost", and it is empty in every row. Column F is a TRUE/FALSE helper whose test is reused in column G. In the same way, column I's IF already contains the condition for "first product with the highest cost". That condition is that this row has the highest cost and it is the first such row counted so far.

So H2 is most likely the TRUE/FALSE version of that condition.

{"formula": "=AND(COUNTIF($B$2:$B2,MAX($B$2:$B$7))=1,B2=MAX($B$2:$B$7))"}

Row 1 holds day numbers 1–24, with day *d* in the column *d* places right of column A. Cell A6 holds today's day of the month. The "last 14 days" run rate should therefore cover days A6−13 through A6 of the "New" row (row 3).

`OFFSET(A3,0,A6-13,1,14)` starts at day A6−13 and spans 14 columns. Dividing that sum by 14 gives the daily average, matching the month-to-date formula in F8.

{"formula": "=SUM(OFFSET(A3,0,A6-13,1,14))/14"}

The "Actual" column G averages the readings that fall in each 6-second window ending at the time in column E. For example, G5 averages 11:00:02 and 11:00:04, and G6 averages 11:00:06 to 11:00:10. So F5 most likely computes the same average automatically. It averages column B where the time is at least 6 seconds before E5 and earlier than E5.

Time values can carry tiny rounding errors, so a reading that lands exactly on a window boundary could occasionally fall into the neighbouring window.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIFS($B$5:$B$370,$A$5:$A$370,\">=\"&(E5-\"0:6.0\"),$A$5:$A$370,\"<\"&E5)"}

D14 is the WEIGHT cell. E14 rounds G47 up using a ceiling of F47, and F14 does the same pattern with G50 and F50. So D14 most likely rounds the computed weight in G40 up to the weight ceiling in F40.

G47 uses D14, but it doesn't depend on D14's own formula, so this creates no circular reference.

{"formula": "=CEILING.MATH(G40,F40)"}

F17 most likely holds the working version of the formula written out as text in G17. H17 shows 4, and this formula returns 4 on the data.

It counts how many numbers in C5:C13 also appear in Data set 1 (F5:H13) in an unbroken run. 33, 57, 71 and 83 all match, so the run length is 4.

{"formula": "=MAX(FREQUENCY(C5:C13,IF(COUNTIF(F5:H13,C5:C13)=0,C5:C13)))-1"}

The cells above (M3:M9) all hold the same formula, so M10 most likely continues that pattern:

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(B$3:B$17,$P$7)"}

P7 is currently empty, so this will count blank cells rather than a shift. If you meant to count a particular shift time, point the reference at a filled cell instead, such as $P$2 or $P$3.

E2 sits where the "Bakers" row meets the "0 - 50" column. The natural formula counts the rows in column A that match the segment in D2 and have an amount between 0 and 50.

`COUNTIFS` handles this, with column and row references anchored so the formula can be filled across and down. The "0 - 50" band is coded as `>=0` and `<=50`.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($A$2:$A$337,$D2,$B$2:$B$337,\">=0\",$B$2:$B$337,\"<=50\")"}

F10 is the "Month start date" step in the workings section. The main formula builds this value as `d`, using `DATE(YEAR($A2),MONTH(1&MID(F$1,4,10)),1)`.

F9 already holds the month name, so F10 can reference it: `MONTH(1&F9)` turns a name like "April" into its month number. Using `$A2` keeps the same row-2 date as the main formula.

