D7 then uses C7 in its lookup.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(B7,$E$6,\"y\")"}

The "Date Value" column most likely pulls the date out of the First Join text in C2, which looks like "11/20/23, 2:02:13 PM". This formula takes the part before the comma and turns it into a date with DATEVALUE.

If column C already holds real date-time numbers rather than text, `=INT(C2)` would be the better choice. DATEVALUE can also misread dates if the system's date order isn't month/day/year.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(LEFT(C2,FIND(\",\",C2)-1))"}

The cell needs a real date pulled from the text in A2 (e.g. "Fri 14 Mar 7:50pm"). This formula drops the weekday and the time, leaving "14 Mar", and turns that into a date. It also handles single-digit days like "1 Mar".

Since the text has no year, Excel will assume the current year. `DATEVALUE("14 Mar")` should work with most regional settings. `TEXTAFTER`/`TEXTBEFORE` need Excel 365 or 2021+.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(TEXTBEFORE(TEXTAFTER(A2,\" \"),\" \",2))"}

Row 3 is labeled "Maximum", and B2:D2 hold the years 2009–2011. So B3 most likely holds the maximum of something for the year 2009.

The best candidate is the V.L balance in column H, filtered to rows where column J (the year) equals B2. Because the balance runs down over the year, the maximum is effectively the starting balance for that year. A MAXIFS formula gives that.

