A 6x installment price per square metre in E12 is most likely the cash price in D12 spread over 6 payments, with interest added. My suggestion uses `PMT` to get each payment, treating the discount rate in C6 as annual and dividing it by 12 for a monthly rate. It then multiplies by the number of payments in C7 to give the total price.

This is a best guess. If C6 is already a monthly rate, the `/12` should be dropped. The formula also assumes payments come at the end of each period; if the first installment is paid upfront, `PMT` would need its type argument set to 1.

{"formula": "=PMT($C$6/12,$C$7,-D12)*$C$7"}

Column G holds hours worked, calculated from the Start time (E8) and End time (F8). `MOD(...,1)` handles shifts that end after midnight, such as 4:00 PM to 12:00 AM. Multiplying by 24 turns the time fraction into a number of hours, which the Regular and O/T columns appear to need.

One caveat: rows where Start and End are both 12:00 AM will show 0 hours, not 24. If those are meant to be full 24-hour shifts, use `=(F8-E8+(F8<=E8))*24` instead.

{"formula": "=MOD(F8-E8,1)*24"}

C23 should mirror C22 but use the decremental rows (20 and 21) instead of the incremental rows (18 and 19).

Decremental values are zero or negative. To get the smallest magnitude to sell, the formula takes MAX of the negative values rather than MIN. The reference ranges are kept exactly as in C22, including its mixed range quirks, with only the row numbers swapped.

{"formula": "=IF($AJ$5=6,IF(OR(C$11<7,C$11>22),0,MAX($I$21:$X$21)),IF($AJ$5=7,MAX($C$20:$Z$20),IF($AJ$5<4,IF(OR(C$11<7,C$11>22),MAX($C20:$H20,$Y20:$Z20),MAX($I20:$X20)),IF($AJ$5=4,IF(OR(C$11<7,C$11>22),MAX($C20:$H21,$X20:$Y21),MAX($I20:$X21)),IF($AJ$5=5,MAX($C20:$Z20,$Y21:$Z21,$C21:$H21),0)))))"}

K19 is labeled "Duplicate % Recovery (<20%)", so it should compare the South average (K11) with the South Duplicate average (K15). The usual measure for duplicates is relative percent difference: the absolute difference divided by the mean of the two.

That gives ABS(K11-K15)/AVERAGE(K11,K15), wrapped in IFERROR so the cell stays blank if either value is missing or non-numeric.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(ABS(K11-K15)/AVERAGE(K11,K15),\"\")"}

